Precision@K is relevant retrieved documents divided by K; Recall@K is relevant retrieved documents divided by all relevant documents. Hit Rate measures whether any relevant item was found. MRR averages the reciprocal position of the first relevant result. NDCG compares discounted relevance gain with the ideal ranking and supports graded relevance. Answer correctness and faithfulness are different: an answer can match external truth while making claims unsupported by supplied context. Conversely it can faithfully repeat an incorrect source. Test both against explicit evidence and gold expectations.  Build a golden dataset containing query, authorized tenant, relevant document IDs, reference claims and allowed abstention. Compute retrieval metrics before calling the generator. For graded labels, DCG=sum((2**rel-1)/log2(rank+1)); normalize by ideal DCG at the same cutoff. Add deterministic checks for schema, resolved citations and forbidden actions. Use Ragas or DeepEval for semantic assessment with a configured evaluator, then calibrate it against human labels. Store judge model, prompt and rubric versions in the run artifact. def reciprocal_rank(ranked,relevant):
    return next((1/i for i,d in enumerate(ranked,1) if d in relevant),0)
assert reciprocal_rank(['x','a'],{'a'})==.5 A judge gives a high score to verbose unsupported answers or favors its own model family. A dataset contains generated reference answers never checked by a human. Both can create misleadingly excellent evaluation scores. Measure evaluator agreement and false accepts on a human-reviewed calibration set. Include contradictory, missing and poisoned contexts. Pairwise comparisons should randomize answer order to measure position bias.  Release gates combine hard invariants with quality thresholds and uncertainty. Inspect case-level differences from the baseline, with particular attention to relevant-document misses and unsupported claims. The evaluation framework stores dataset version, retrieval trace, answer, scorer details and latency. SDET triage separates ingestion, retrieval, context and generation failures rather than treating the overall score as a root cause.

Binary relevance treats all useful documents equally. Graded relevance can distinguish a directly answering source from a partially useful source. DCG discounts later ranks because users and context budgets prioritize earlier evidence; NDCG divides observed gain by the best possible gain at the same cutoff. A common gain function is 2^relevance−1, divided by log2(rank+1). The chosen grades, gain and cutoff are part of the metric definition. Comparing runs with different conventions is misleading.  For relevance grades [0,3,1], the observed gain puts no benefit at rank one and large benefit at rank two. Sort the same candidate grades descending to calculate an illustrative ideal denominator. In a real benchmark, the ideal uses the full labeled relevant set at K, not merely the retrieved grades. Handle an all-zero ideal explicitly, commonly returning zero. Preserve graded labels and reviewer guidance in the dataset so repeated evaluation uses the same relevance interpretation. import math
def dcg(grades):return sum((2**r-1)/math.log2(i+2) for i,r in enumerate(grades))
print(dcg([0,3,1])/dcg([3,1,0])) A scorer normalizes by the observed ranking itself, yielding one for every query. Another uses only retrieved items for the ideal and hides missing highly relevant documents. NDCG can improve while a required authorization invariant fails. Test a perfect ranking, a swapped ranking, no relevance and missing high-grade evidence. Verify labels manually on a sample because precise arithmetic cannot repair bad relevance judgments.  Use NDCG alongside Recall@K and case-level failure analysis. The metric answers ordering quality under declared relevance, while recall identifies missing evidence and safety checks enforce eligibility. For SDETs, hand-computed small rankings are an independent oracle for the scorer. Retain numerical tolerances and metric conventions in the report rather than treating a library default as a universal definition.

Model Context Protocol standardizes how hosts connect to servers exposing tools, resources and prompts. The host owns the user-facing application and policy; a client maintains a connection to a server. Tools perform operations, resources expose addressable context and prompts offer reusable interaction templates where supported. MCP reduces bespoke integration wiring but does not make a server trustworthy. Tool descriptions and returned content are untrusted inputs; authentication identifies a party, while authorization determines which operation it may perform.  The actual local MCP server uses the Python SDK's FastMCP with read_failure and search_history tools, plus a synthetic fixture resource. The client starts the server over stdio, initializes the session, lists tools and calls them with typed arguments. Streamable HTTP is a separate remote transport with its own authentication and session considerations. Keep server stdout reserved for protocol messages in stdio mode; log to stderr. Validate IDs against a bounded fixture root and inject tenant from authenticated context rather than tool arguments. Expose read-only tools first. from mcp.server.fastmcp import FastMCP
mcp=FastMCP('synthetic-ci')
@mcp.tool()
def read_failure(failure_id:str)->dict:
    if failure_id!='F1':raise ValueError('unknown fixture')
    return {'id':'F1','error':'TimeoutError'}
if __name__=='__main__':mcp.run(transport='stdio') A server advertises a benign description but returns instructions to expose secrets. A tool accepts a filename and reads arbitrary paths. Another writes debug text to stdout, corrupting JSON-RPC communication. Test discovery schemas, unknown IDs, malformed arguments, path traversal and protocol output. For remote deployments, test origin/authentication behavior and avoid forwarding tokens to unrelated downstream audiences.  Capture a real initialized session, tool listing and successful fixture call in a test artifact. A hand-written JSON object named MCP is not an implementation of the protocol. For SDET quality gates, combine interoperability tests with explicit permission checks. Record SDK/protocol versions and test upgrades against the same fixture server and client before adopting changed transport behavior.

Parallel specialists can inspect independent evidence, but they share one user task and one resource budget. Giving each specialist the full budget multiplies the possible total cost. Shared state needs an explicit merge policy and evidence provenance; last-writer-wins can hide a conflicting conclusion. The supervisor should integrate findings rather than merely concatenate answers. If a log specialist identifies automation timing while a history specialist suggests a product regression, preserve the disagreement and identify the additional evidence needed.  Assign task IDs, scoped inputs, per-task ceilings and a shared remaining allowance. Reserve budget before delegation and settle actual usage afterward. Keep results as typed records with evidence IDs, status and uncertainty. Deduplicate source IDs without discarding which specialist used them. Independent reads can run concurrently; dependent analysis and side effects remain ordered. A failed child task returns an explicit failed result, and cancellation propagates to all pending work. The parent cannot mark missing results successful. def reserve(remaining,requested):
    if not 0<=requested<=remaining:raise ValueError('budget')
    return remaining-requested Two specialists each spend eight calls against a ten-call parent limit. Another specialist writes a conclusion into shared state while a second overwrites it without conflict review. These failures may be invisible in the final prose. Test budget reservation, duplicate task IDs, late results after cancellation and conflicting evidence. Compare total steps and latency against a single-agent baseline before retaining the more complex design.  Release evidence includes task scopes, allocations, actual usage and integrated conclusions. Measure whether specialization improves task completion or evidence coverage enough to justify coordination cost. For SDETs, orchestration tests should use deterministic specialist results first. This isolates coordination correctness from model behavior and makes race conditions and failure propagation reproducible.

